          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "Your total cholesterol result is 6.4, but I need to know the unit. Is it mmol/L or mg/dL? The two units give very different numbers, so the unit changes what the result means.\n\nI could not find the cutoffs for total cholesterol categories (such as desirable, borderline, or high) in the guidance I can search. So I can't tell you which category 6.4 falls into.\n\nThe guidance I did find says that a total cholesterol value is not read on its own:\n\n- **Screening uses a full lipid panel:** total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides. A non-fasting sample is acceptable for initial screening in most adults. (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary)\n- **Risk, not one number, drives decisions:** Estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk uses age, sex, total and HDL cholesterol, systolic blood pressure, blood pressure treatment, diabetes and smoking status. (Same guidance)\n- **Other factors can change the picture:** Family history of early heart disease, chronic kidney disease, persistently raised triglycerides, and metabolic syndrome can all affect how a result is interpreted. (Same guidance)\n\nA useful next step is to ask your clinician or the lab which category your result falls into, and to ask how it fits with your LDL, HDL and triglyceride results and your other risk factors. If you tell me the unit and the other values on your panel, I can explain what the guidance says about them, though I can't diagnose or tell you what to do about them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
